<p>Earlier this month, Apple rolled out its <a href="https://sixcolors.com/link/2026/11/siri-ai-keeps-on-improving/">Siri AI Developer Preview</a>, which lays the groundwork for developers to allow their apps to be controlled in a fairly sophisticated fashion by Apple&#8217;s AI assistant.</p>
<p>To say this is an important development would be a colossal understatement. The execution of this feature is the core of Apple&#8217;s game plan for the next few years, when it intends to release numerous products that leverage its iPhone app ecosystem to power Siri AI.</p>
<p>No pressure&#8230;</p>
<p>Apple&#8217;s latest new product, the <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/homeview-review-a-late-arrival-with-promise-but-still-showing-some-seams/">HomeView</a>, is built around Siri AI. (It&#8217;s a light dash of Siri AI right now, but its links with content on individual iPhones show what the company is envisioning there.) The new <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/10/a-299-apple-tv-is-that-all-there-is/">Apple TV 4K</a> has added microphones to make it an additional home access point for Siri AI.</p>
<p>And there&#8217;s much more to come, as reported by <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/newsletters/2026-11-15/apple-siri-ai-app-integrations-power-smart-glasses-iphone-duo-2-face-id-plan">Mark Gurman at Bloomberg</a>:</p>
<blockquote><p>
  Apple needs software makers to begin preparing for what it hopes will be its flagship new product of 2027: <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/newsletters/2026-05-31/apple-glasses-late-2027-release-watch-comparison-ios-28-apple-tv-homepod">smart glasses</a> built around Siri AI, code-named N50.</p>
<p>  Apple is counting on integration with the apps people already use on their iPhones to be a key differentiator for the glasses, I’m told. The idea is to make those apps available through Siri, allowing users to get things done without grabbing their phones. Apple believes its large developer ecosystem could give it an advantage over Meta Platforms Inc. and Alphabet Inc.’s Google, potentially providing countless third-party integrations when the glasses go on sale.
</p></blockquote>
<p>Gurman expects the Siri-powered glasses (which are basically AirPods you wear on your face) to be <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/newsletters/2026-07-26/apple-glasses-may-debut-at-wwdc-2027-privacy-camera-features-versus-meta-ms1v7lta">released at WWDC</a>, with a similar set of AirPods armed with AI-powered cameras set to debut later in the year. (<a href="https://www.threads.com/@imdavidpierce/post/DfxU1egHQCS?xmt=AQG0N9eRuCQqgfKAonf7cMonG8l7FK001cl-ie3eYNw6mbLOOqztzjnv-qOkpbwa7tRSGUKc&#038;slof=1">We&#8217;ll see</a>.)</p>
<p>These devices don&#8217;t make a lot of sense unless there&#8217;s a useful AI assistant behind them. Siri AI is not quite that yet—it&#8217;s more reliable and more useful than the old Siri was, but it&#8217;s not as good as it should be, and it&#8217;s sometimes very slow—but at least it&#8217;s promising. Still, just as the iPhone would be a lot less appealing if it only ran Apple-built apps, Siri AI is less appealing if it doesn&#8217;t have access to the functionality of the app ecosystem.</p>
<p>Currently, Siri AI can use Spotlight to search content offered by third-party apps, but what it can&#8217;t do is learn about functionality offered by individual apps and command those apps to perform those functions in service of a larger request. That&#8217;s the goal of the Siri AI Developer Preview—and that sort of stuff is what&#8217;s going to make the HomeView (let alone glasses or AirPods paired with an iPhone) a more appealing product.</p>
<p>If Apple <em>can&#8217;t</em> pull this off, well, it calls into question the trajectory of Apple&#8217;s entire future product strategy. There&#8217;s no point in making amazing hardware if there&#8217;s nothing running on it that appeals.</p>
